{ "answer": ["thalassemia","alpha thalassemia","beta thalassemia"], "clues": ["During the Neolithic Revolution (around 10,000 to 7,000 years ago), humans transitioned from mobile hunter-gatherers to settled farmers, leading to a change in disease exposure. Over millenia, the frequency of the gene causing this disorder rose dramatically, specifically in the Mediterranean basin, the Middle East, the Indian subcontinent, and Southeast Asia","This condition is now diagnosed around the world, brought by the Ottoman Empire expansion, Arab Conquests, and centuries of migration. Because of this, medical systems around the world now routinely screen for this as a standard part of prenatal or neonatal care.","Split into alpha and beta, carrying a singly copy of the gene can offer partial protection against severe malaria."] },
{ "answer": ["scoliosis"], "clues": ["Early treatments for this condition prescribed by Hippocrates included pulling patients on wooden ladders or binding them tightly to sticks","In the early 1800s, French surgeons used mechanical traction to treat this condition which caused so many cases of paralysis that the Royal Academy of Medicine intervened, discredited the methods, and forced the creation of a specialized research commission","The name of this comes from an ancient Greek term meaning 'bend'. Around 90% of affected children do not need surgery and only require routine monitoring or a back brace during growth spurts"] },
{ "answer": ["ketamine"], "clues": ["Chemists synthesized phencyclidine (PCP) in 1956, which worked as a powerful anesthetic but caused severe side effects like agitation, hallucinations, and post-operative delirium. In 1962, this drug was synthesized providing the benefits of PCP but with far fewer side effects","This drug was the primary field anesthetic for US military medics in Vietnam. Unlike other sedatives, this did not suppress breathing or dangerously lower blood pressure meaning medics could safely administer it via an injection without needing bulky ventilators or intubation gear.","In 2000, a team at Yale University gave a single, low-dose infusion of this NDMA antagonist to patients with severe, treatment-resistant depression, leading to improvement in depression symptoms within hours. Because it can cause hallucinogenic and out-of-body dissociative effects, it is sometimes used recreationally."] },
{ "answer": ["Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","SBP"], "clues": ["This condition was first noted in medical literature by Da Bozzolo in 1885 although not named until the 1960-70s. At that time, prognosis was poor with mortality rates approaching 90% although now in hospital mortality is around 20-40%.","Ludwig van Beethoven had this condition and died March 26, 1827 during a sudden, violent thunderstorm. Dr. Johannes Wagner performed an autopsy the next day on the kitchen table where the affected area of the body held four quarts of turbid, reddish, and cloudy fluid.","This condition is typically caused by a single organism and is diagnosed based on number of polymorphonuclear neutrophils in the fluid"] },
{ "answer": ["Autism","autism spectrum disorder","autism spectrum","ASD","asperger"], "clues": ["This was not recognized as a diagnosis until 1980 and currently divided into three levels affecting about 1 in 127 people globally","For decades in the 1950s-70s, a popular theory was that this was caused by a lack of maternal warmth or emotionally cold and detached mothers who failed to form a proper bond","Albert Einstein exhibited childhood speech delays, intense hyper-focus on mathematics, and a strong preference for a solitary lifestyle. Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart showed  extreme sensitivities to loud noises and struggled with impulse control and social appropriateness. Some theorize they may have had this neurodevelopmental difference although they cannot be diagnosed retrospectively."] },
{ "answer": ["amputation","leg amputation","AKA","BKA"], "clues": ["In the middle ages, this was primarily performed by barbers, particularly during wartime. This is part of the origin of the red and white striped barber pole where the red represents blood, white represents bandages, and the pole represents the wooden staff or rod that patients gripped tightly. Barbers returned strictly to grooming and hair care in 1745.","You may have heard of the famous surgery of Dr. Liston. Before modern anesthesia and antiseptic hygiene, a surgeon's speed meant the difference between life and death on the operating table. According to medical lore, during one procedure, Dr. Liston accidentally slashed off his assistant's fingers and the patient and assistant both died of gangrene. A panicked spectator in the crowd reportedly dropped dead of shock resulting in a 300% mortality from a single procedure. What was that procedure?","While vascular diseases such as diabetes and peripheral artery disease drive the majority of these procedures in developed populations, trauma remains a leading driver globally. Roughly 80% of people who have undergone this procedure experience phantom sensations."] },
{ "answer": ["coronary artery disease","CAD","heart attack","MI","myocardial infarction","acute MI","acute myocardial infarction","AMI"], "clues": ["CT scans of ancient Egyptian mummies from as far back as 1580 BC show clear evidence of this disease. Research across 4,000 years of ancient populations found that nearly 35-45% of examined mummies had signs of this","During WWII, Nazi confiscation of livestock in countries like Norway forced populations onto plant-heavy, low-meat diets. National data showed a dramatic drop in this symptomatic manifestations of this disease during the occupation, which surged again after meat and dairy returned in 1945","This has been the leading cause of death in the USA since 1990. Much of the modern medical understanding of this disease is based on the Framingham study which is ongoing with third-generation participants."] },
{ "answer": ["fluconazole","diflucan"], "clues": ["This was fast-tracked through the US FDA after a short nine-month review window due to the global HIV/AIDS epidemic to help treat severe, life-threatening opportunistic infections","This drug replaces the imidazole ring from a similar drug with a triazole ring which stops the drug from binding aggressively to human liver enzymes and reduces the risk of severe hepatotoxicity.","While this has a serum half-life of about 30 hours, this drug can linger in the body for up to 10 days which is why a single dose is often sufficient to treat a standard vaginal yeast infection."] },
