{ "answer": ["Metolazone","zaroxolyn"], "clues": ["In a 2020 study, scientists screened an extensive library of existing, off-patent drugs to see if any could combat aging. They discovered that this drug significantly extends the lifespan of wild-type C. elegans otherwise known as the roundworm through activating the mitochondrial repair pathway.","Though this drug behaves similarly to thiazide diuretics, it is technically classified as a quinazoline-sulfonamide. Unlike true thiazides that lose effectiveness as the patient's kidney function drops, this drug maintains its potency even in advanced chronic kidney disease.","In the mid-20th century, the discovery of powerful loop diuretics revolutionized the treatment of severe fluid retention. However, doctors quickly ran into a major obstacle where a patient’s kidneys adapt to loop diuretics and stop responding. The introduction of this drug provided a major clinical breakthrough, as its unique chemical structure allowed it to be paired sequentially with loop diuretics to break through this resistance and safely restart fluid removal"] },
{ "answer": ["urinary incontinence","overactive bladder","OAB","neurogenic bladder","overflow incontinence","urge incontinence","incontinence","bladder incontinence"], "clues": ["The earliest written mentions of this appear in Egyptian medical papyri, which explicitly describe it resulting from spinal cord injuries. There are numerous other causes including surgery, infection, tumors, or muscle weakness","In 1747, Lorenz Heister introduced a perineal compression belt designed to mechanically stop this from happening. Other treatments around that time included opium, atropine, and paraffin injections","This affects millions of people worldwide and is about twice as common in women. Some common types include urge, stress, overflow, and functional"] },
{ "answer": ["Ticagrelor","brilinta"], "clues": ["Scientists used the chemical blueprint of ATP as their starting point for developing this drug, creating a new chemical class called cyclopentyl-triazolo-pyrimidines (CPTPs)","Despite showing superior reduction in cardiovascular deaths compared to clopidogrel globally in its landmark PLATO trial (2009), the data showed this drug was highly effective everywhere in the world except North America. They found that North American doctors routinely prescribed a higher daily maintenance dose of aspirin which chemically interfered with this drug's efficacy. This led to a permanent boxed warning stating it should only be taking with a low-dose baby aspirin","Before this drug, all standard oral P2Y12 inhibitors were irreversible. This was the first approved reversible oral antiplatelet agent and is currently preferred over clopidogrel for most patients with acute coronary syndrome"] },
